# claude: every exclude pattern below is written '**/xxx' on purpose.
# Unlike .gitignore, a bare 'xxx.o' pattern here only matches at the
# build context root -- it will NOT reach nested paths like
# tests/s/mini/hello_arm.exe, which is where these files actually
# live. Dropping the '**/' silently turns these excludes into no-ops.
/Dockerfile
.git

# partial copy of .gitignore
**/*.o
**/*.a
**/*.[5678iv]
**/*.ao
# pad's test files -- without these, a leftover local build of e.g.
# tests/s/mini/hello_macos_arm64.exe gets copied into the build
# context via COPY, and mk sees the target already up to date and
# skips its recipe -- silently masking recipe bugs (like a broken
# `if` as a recipe's last line) that a genuinely clean CI checkout
# would catch.
**/*.exe
**/*.uexe
# same reasoning as *.exe above: benchs/compcert/mkfile's %.goken
# binaries are just as vulnerable to this stale-artifact/mtime trap --
# worse, actually, since %.goken's mtime check can't tell one cputype's
# build apart from another's (same filename regardless of target arch),
# so a leftover mips-targeted foo.goken silently "looks done" to a
# later arm64 build too, not just skipping a recipe but running the
# wrong architecture's binary under qemu/binfmt without any indication.
**/*.goken

# claude: golden/reference binaries checked into git for byte-for-byte
# `cmp` comparisons (variants tests) and GO's elf testdata -- must
# survive the blanket excludes above now that they actually apply
# recursively.
!tests/s/variants/expected/**
!tests/c/variants/expected/**
!GO/pkg/debug/elf/testdata/**
